Phnom Penh Capital Administration Holds a Workshop to Demonstrate Transport Master Plan in Phnom Penh
(Phnom Penh): The Phnom Penh Administration on June 21, 2019 organized a consultative workshop on the draft master plan for transportation in Phnom Penh under the chairmanship of Mr. Nuon Pharna, Deputy Governor and represented by Mr. Khuong Sreng, Governor of Phnom Penh. Attended by local authorities, administrative districts of 14 districts and 105 communes, national and international experts from universities and development partners.
Mr. Suy Serith, Deputy Governor of Phnom Penh, said that the workshop was held to gather input to update the master plan, which started in 2001 and ended in 2005 with technical assistance from the Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA). ) But this plan has not yet been officially approved. This plan is for Phnom Penh in 2035. Traffic and transportation problems, if we do not solve them now, it will be more complicated.
He said that Phnom Penh currently has 115 traffic lights and cameras that are connected to an intelligent control system that is able to automatically set red and blue lights according to the number of passengers and the presence of delegates. But the population density has risen sharply and the traffic situation has been complicated by lawless behavior. Phnom Penh has grown by removing more than 20 communes from Kandal province. The bus currently has nearly 30,000 daily users.
Updating and approving the master plan will serve as a document to mobilize funding and support from relevant ministries to implement the master plan successfully.
JICA President Shukano Yuichi confirmed that the study was completed in 2014, identifying several priority projects, including the provision of 80 buses and the installation of intelligent traffic management systems and traffic control and monitoring centers. We hope that all parties will participate in the successful implementation of this master plan.
He said that we come together today to know that after the completion of this master plan, our friends and good partners, especially we have studied together to implement and monitor the implementation together.
Masato Koto, a JICA expert, said that according to the study, sidewalks and sidewalks were used for parking or shopping, and travel speeds decreased significantly as demand for travel increased faster than human growth.
(Phnom Penh): "Officials only have names, never come to work" is a word that is often mentioned in various ministries and institutions of the state. The head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, once reminded the ministries and institutions to integrate technology to manage the attendance list and monitor officials who do not come to work.
Respecting the spirit of Samdech Techo Hun Sen and participating in eliminating the activities of "only names do not come to work" officials, the Ministry of Public Works and Transport has been launching an automated system to control the attendance list and monitor the officials who do not come to work.
Recently, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, chaired a meeting to discuss the establishment of an automated system to manage the presence of civil servants.
Heang Sotheayut, spokesman for the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, told Fresh News on June 21, 2019: "The meeting was held to strengthen the management mechanism and monitor the presence of civil servants under the Ministry to determine the basis for Provide monthly benefits to encourage officials who come to work regularly.
According to Mr. Sothea Yut, the meeting also discussed the timing of fingerprint scanning, reports and notifications via smart technology to the parties to be able to check immediately on a smartphone or tablet.
Through that meeting, the Senior Minister agreed that the Department in charge of preparing official regulations on the implementation of the system for managing the presence of civil servants and training on how to use the system to all units under the Ministry to be better.
Mr. Heang Sotheayut added that the construction of this automation system is aimed at better management of the attendance list of officials, while the current registration on the attendance book is not very accurate. In other words, the use of this attendance book requires people to memorize the responsibilities of each unit on a regular basis, so if you end up working late at night and forget to sign in the evening, you will definitely be absent.
In particular, the automation system that the Ministry is re-establishing makes it much easier to register and manage data. Mr. Heang Sotheayut said that this automated system allows officers to scan their presence at any time, even if the officer leaves work late at night.
In addition, the automation system connected to the data center using modern technology can allow the Ministry leadership to immediately know the presence of how many officials are working and how many are not coming to work. In particular, this automation system will automatically send reports via telegram at the end of working hours.
Mr. Sothea Yut expressed confidence that the automated attendance system would work better to manage the attendance of officers and also help eliminate officers who evade work. In particular, it helps the Ministry find officials who act to provide monthly incentives. Stay away from drugs! Nationwide anti-drug campaign on June 21: 16 cases cracked, 40 arrested
(Phnom Penh): The nationwide campaign against illegal drugs for June 21, 2019 showed that law enforcement forces cracked down on 16 cases of illegal drugs, arresting 40 suspects.
The Senate will convene a meeting of its Standing Committee early next week to decide on six agendas.
(Phnom Penh): The Senate of Cambodia will hold a meeting of the Standing Committee on June 24, 2019 to review and decide on six important agendas at the Senate. This is according to the announcement of the General Secretariat of the Senate, which was received by Fresh News on June 21, 2019.
According to the announcement of the General Secretariat of the Senate, the six main agendas include:
1. Examine and decide on the request of the Ninth Committee of the Senate to include the draft law on the management of oil and petroleum products in the agenda of the third session of the Senate of the fourth legislature.
2. Examine and decide on the request of the Third Committee of the Senate to submit the draft law on the approval of the agreement between the Royal Government of the Kingdom of Cambodia and the Government of the People's Republic of Bangladesh on the promotion and mutual protection of investment. Into the agenda of the third session of the Senate of the fourth legislature.
3. Review and decide on the request of the Third Committee of the Senate to put the draft law on the approval of the agreement between the Kingdom of Cambodia and the United Arab Emirates on the promotion and mutual protection of investment into the agenda. Third session of the Senate of the fourth legislature.
4. Examine and decide on the request of the Third Committee of the Senate to submit the draft law on the approval of the agreement between the Royal Government of the Kingdom of Cambodia and the Government of the Republic of Turkey on the promotion and mutual protection of investment. In the agenda of the third session of the Senate of the fourth legislature.
5. Examine and decide on the request of the Seventh Committee of the Senate to submit the draft law on the approval of the agreement on the establishment of the International Network for Bamboo and Rattan to be included in the agenda of the third session of the Senate for the fourth term.
6. Set the agenda and date for the resumption of the third session of the Senate of the fourth legislature.
