(Phnom Penh): Pine trees grow mostly in Southeast Asia and in countries with a tropical climate. In Cambodia, most of the trees grow in the mountains and highlands of the north of the country, including Siem Reap, Oddar Meanchey and Preah Vihear.
According to the dictionary of Samdech Sangkhareach Chuon Nath Sralao, it is the name of a large tree, similar in shape to Trabek Prey, but the tree is bigger than the flesh, it is more beautiful and can be used for many things: Sralao board, Saulao cabinet and Sralao table. Khmer people like to use pine trees to make tableware, cabinets, chairs, beds, boats and wooden items for making boats. Some Cambodians also cut and cut pine trees to make boards and wood for building houses.
According to the ancient customs and beliefs of the people of Svay Leu in Siem Reap and Kulen districts of Preah Vihear province, when the locals prepare to build a new house, the owner must have 12 kinds of wood, including sralao, to make furniture such as pillars, planks, planks Floor mats to make the homeowner rich and prosperous.
Because Cambodians have a long tradition of believing in culture and living with nature for a long time, some ancient Cambodian writers and scholars have named the tree after the village or community in which they lived. A song that he wrote and wrote in proverbs to educate and teach people, with the words "Do not bend, do not rebuke the corrupt."
In the Khmer song, the lyricist Kong Bunthoeun also composed a song called "Mlup Sneh Deum Sralao". Before his death, Kong Bunthoeun described the history of composing this song as follows: In 1973, I wrote a novel entitled "Pailin Tide".
