Taking this opportunity, please share the history of the establishment of this regional organization:
1: History of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, established on August 8, 1967. Initially, there were five countries: Malaysia, Indonesia, the Philippines, Singapore and Thailand. The founding countries, with the exception of Thailand, were all newly occupied territories from the yoke of colonialism at the time.
Coupled with the onset of the Cold War, the five countries are urged to form an association with the common goal of promoting peace, stability, economic growth and social and cultural development throughout Southeast Asia. Since 1984, the other five countries in the region include Brunei, Vietnam, Laos, Myanmar and Cambodia (April 30, 1999).
2. ASEAN Secretariat The ASEAN Secretariat is based in Jakarta, Indonesia, with the Secretary-General in charge of coordinating and implementing ASEAN activities. The Secretary-General shall be appointed by the ASEAN Summit for a term of five years, which shall be rotated according to the alphabetical order of the English names of the Member States.
Article 31: ASEAN Chairmanship Article 31 of the ASEAN Charter states, "The ASEAN Chairmanship shall be rotated annually in the alphabetical order of the English names of the Member States" and the Member States holding the ASEAN Chairmanship shall chair the Meeting. ASEAN Summit and other meetings. Cambodia has twice chaired ASEAN, the first in 2002 and the second in 2012.
5. ASEAN Meetings All major decisions related to the destiny of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) are made by the ASEAN Summit, which takes place annually in the ASEAN Chairmanship. In addition to the summit, there are other meetings, such as ministerial meetings by sector.
8. The ASEAN Foundation was established in 1961 as the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASA), a group of participating countries such as the Philippines, Malaysia and Thailand. ASEAN was established on August 8, 1967, when the foreign ministers of five countries - Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore and Thailand - signed the ASEAN Treaty, commonly known as the Bangkok Treaty.
