The review of the CDI decision was made at the second meeting of the Working Group on the Establishment of the Research Institute of the Center for International Democrats (CDI) held in Phnom Penh on June 20, 2019.
Cambodia is a post-conflict country and one of the least developed countries that has benefited from these trade preferences. About 1 million factory workers, especially women from rural areas working in the textile and garment sector, access to the European market without tariff barriers and quotas.
In February 2019, the European Union announced the start of proceedings that could lead to a temporary suspension of the preferential treatment. The EBA system may be abolished if the beneficiary is found to be violating important human and labor rights. This is according to the announcement of the European Commission released on February 11, 2019.
In early June, EU representatives returned to Cambodia to review the situation in Cambodia regarding the preferential treatment. In a recent meeting with Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Cambodia-Europe affirmed its commitment to continue working to prevent Cambodia from losing the EBA.
The Center for International Democrats (CDI) states: "We must realize that democratization is a long way off. The rise of Cambodia's middle class will play an important role in strengthening democratic values and building democratic institutions. The EBA has made significant contributions to the foundations of democracy in Cambodia. ”
The CDI also called on the EU to work responsibly on deciding whether to withdraw the EBA from Cambodia. Economic sanctions should not be used as a political tool.
