Chinese Ambassador to Cambodia: Overcoming the Underground Monsters Hidden in the Dark (Phnom Penh): Mine is a demon that takes over human life. According to an incomplete statistic, the world currently has more than 110 million landmines in more than 70 countries, and more than 20,000 people are injured and killed every year.
Some of the mines have been left over since World War II, and many of the minefields are not clear today, posing a serious threat. Some have said that the scars of war are the hardest to remove, not the collapsed houses and broken bridges, but the landmines.
Landmines remain an obstacle to Cambodia's nation-building and economic development. During the past 40 years, landmines have injured and killed more than 50,000 Cambodians, including more than 30 in 2018 alone. I once read a news article about a family of three living on the outskirts of Phnom Penh. Rangqi, a husband, wakes up at 4 am every day and starts running 8 km.
When the sun shines on the earth, he takes off his prosthetic leg, replaces it with a rubber prosthesis, and begins his new day. More than 10 years ago, when his wife went on a mining trip with her family, she was trampled by a landmine and lost her right leg. Every time I hear about mine casualties, I am reminded of this news and feel very sad about the hardships of those families.
