Cambodian human rights chief insists action against former opposition party does not restrict freedom of expression (Video inside) (Phnom Penh) Opposition by the court in the past was not a restriction on the voice of opposition or a restriction on freedom of expression, but an application of the laws and court procedures in force in Cambodia.
Mr. Keo Remy stressed that as a rule of law and universal human rights, gatherings or expressions of malicious intent to support illegal movements to provoke unrest and overthrow a legitimate government in a sovereign state are not the right to freedom of association. Gatherings and speeches are protected by law, but instead have the legal responsibility to follow the court procedures in force in that sovereign state.
Contrary to the claims of the two Special Rapporteurs, freedom of assembly and expression is fully guaranteed and protected in Cambodia, with the vast majority of the people freely using it. The media and social media to express their opinions legally.
In particular, VOA was recently allowed to reopen its office and broadcast in Cambodia after completing all legal procedures and procedures in force. .
