South Korea prepares to pack 50,000 tonnes of rice to be sent by UN to North Korea
SEOUL: South Korea on Wednesday said it would arrange to send about 50,000 tonnes of rice through the United Nations (UN) to North Korea to help Pyongyang cope with UN food shortages. Claimed to be getting worse. This was reported by Yonhap News Agency on Wednesday evening, June 19, 2019.
According to the South Korean Ministry of National Unification, the food aid will be shipped to North Korea through the United Nations World Food Program (WFP). It is also the first time South Korea has provided rice aid to North Korea since 2010, when Seoul donated 5,000 tons of rice to help Pyongyang recover from the devastation. Severe flooding.
According to the latest UN report, North Korea has been facing worsening food shortages due to international sanctions over Pyongyang's nuclear program and years of unfavorable conditions. Of nature.
Recently, the World Food Program (WFP) and the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations released a report saying that North Korea's crop yields last year were the lowest since 2008, with about 10 million people. About 40 percent of North Korea's population is in need of food.
