Experts also raise other issues that the multi-layered chiffon on the hands and degrees of the statue (shoulders) also leads to the peeling of the pigment on the body of the statue, because some pigments that have not been painted on the next generation. Good quality as the original paint, which is easy to damage.
* History of the repair of the statue:
Despite being hundreds of years old through many eras of the nation, the royal statues still appear strong and full of power. This angel has also been repaired many times by national and international experts who have suffered due to the natural factors of its age, civil war and harassment by unscrupulous traders attempting to cut off their heads for sale.
Regarding the conservation, Long Nary, a stone conservation expert of the Department of Temple Preservation in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology and a former contributor to the restoration of Taraj, said that so far, Ta Reach has gone through several generations of restoration history. Based on the evidence of conservation techniques and materials in the body that have been repaired from the past.
Nary confirmed that the repair of the star was from the end of the 16th century on both legs, which broke into two pieces, because the experience of experts found that the stone on the legs and body are of different quality. This division was probably due to the transition from Brahmanism to Buddhism.
Later in the French era, seeing the risk of the statue tilting backwards after the fall, French School of the Far East experts also repaired by excavating the soil to strengthen the foundation to keep Vishnu or Taraja more stable this year. 1946
Later, in 1987-1993, in collaboration with the Angkor Conservation of the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts with India, parts of both hands were repaired with cement instead.
Recalling in 1985, Ta Reach's hair was cut off by a group of criminals for the purpose of trafficking, but was unsuccessful. . In chaos, the nation was not yet fully secure, and Angkor conservationists copied the new head from cement to replace the original head to meet the physical characteristics of the statue and the beliefs of the Cambodian people.
In 2002-2003, under the cooperation between the APSARA Authority and the German Institution (GACP), two left and one right and one right shoulder were repaired, which could cause serious damage to the statue and tourists. Passing through here, the team replaced the three arms and shoulders made of cement with sandstone.
