On that occasion, the Ambassador also invited Dr. Governor and his team to visit Malaysia to learn and exchange experiences with each other in the housing development project of the Malaysian company in Tbong Khmum. Senior official of the Ministry of Agriculture: Cambodia has not yet experienced the problem of desertification, only some decline in soil quality
(Kampot): Mr. Meas Piseth, Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and a key figure in the United Nations Convention against Deforestation and Degradation of Land, claims that Cambodia is not currently affected by desertification. However, there is a decline in soil quality due to improper land use and deforestation.
Mr. Meas Piseth added that the decline in soil quality is happening all over the country today, which requires measures to prevent and restore soil quality, if not clear, it will cause serious impact on agricultural products. As a solution, the Ministry of Agriculture, a government agency, is setting up a working group to work on two key strategies to prevent soil degradation.
The official of the Ministry of Agriculture said that currently Cambodia has not been affected by deforestation, only the problem of declining soil quality in some areas due to improper and excessive land use.
In addition, Mr. Mam Amnat also mentioned that deforestation or deterioration of soil quality, which leads to the decline or loss of economic status of farmland, grasslands and forests, often occurs due to the loss of forests and forest ecosystems. Excessive or over-capacity of soil, monoculture, overuse of fertilizers and pesticides, or improper use of natural techniques and factors, especially climate change.
He said that these issues have attracted the global attention, especially of the national and international community, to the economic, social and environmental impacts that affect more than 250 million people around the world. Touch this. In fact, Cambodia is also suffering from climate change, such as global warming, drought and hurricanes, which are increasing today.
"We now recognize that desertification or declining soil quality has not been adequately cared for by the world, although many scientists and countries claim that humanity can remain silent or fight," he said. Against that problem. Especially by strengthening community participation and cooperation at all levels.
