Over the past five years, more than 200,000 Cambodians have been able to improve their skills, but the labor market still lacks skilled workers
(Phnom Penh): In the last 5 years, more than 200,000 trainees, students and workers have been trained to improve their technical and vocational skills, and they have also seized good job opportunities and created jobs. By yourself. This is according to the message celebrating the 2nd National Day of Technical and Vocational Education and Training of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
"In the last five years, more than 200,000 trainees, students and workers have been trained to improve their skills," he said in a statement.
However, Cambodia is currently experiencing a mismatch between skills and insufficient supply of skills due to the limited education and training system and the inability to respond to the rapidly changing workforce needs.
Moreover, technological advances during the Fourth Industrial Revolution will lead to job losses and the creation of new types of jobs. But those who lose their jobs at this stage of development will not be able to find a job if they do not receive retraining and skills development.
In response to the above, Samdech Techo Prime Minister presented some solutions:
* Cambodia has no choice but to increase the professional capacity of the workforce, increase productivity, narrow the gap between skills and insufficient supply of skills through the expansion of labor market information services and employment services to improve the quality of education and vocational training. To maximize the benefits and minimize the negative impacts of the changing world of work and to respond to future skills needs.
* In order to provide flexibility in line with the real situation of the people, training programs are organized for short and long term in the system and outside the system and are implemented in community and mobile enterprises.
* Practical skills training through the training of apprentices in enterprises and establishments is continuously implemented and expanded in accordance with the Labor Law.
* The Royal Government has provided more scholarships to young people who drop out of school, children of the poor, women, the disabled, orphans, indigenous peoples, workers who have lost their jobs and the vulnerable to have access to vocational training. And increase professional skills. Dormitories for teachers, trainees, poor students and women in school are being built in successive training facilities.
* Strengthening relations with industry and internships in the workplace, implementing the quality assurance system and strengthening governance in the field of technical and vocational education and training. So far, 20 public institutions have been recognized by the International Standards Organization. (ISO). Cambodian PM Encourages Citizens to Learn Technical Skills to Get Good Jobs and Increase Income
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, called on people to turn their attention to technical and vocational education in order to seize the good job opportunities that the labor market is in high demand now and in the future. Aims to increase household income to grow.
Samdech Techo's appeal was confirmed in the congratulatory message on the 2nd "National Day of Technical and Vocational Education and Training" under the theme "TVET possible" held on June 15-16. 2019 at Koh Pich Trade Fair Building, Phnom Penh.
In the same message, Samdech Techo Hun Sen called on all ministries, institutions, authorities at all levels, professors, teachers, students, parents, guardians, employers, workers, private sector, NGOs, development partners and compatriots to participate in a number of things. Contains:
1. Participate in maintaining peace and stability in Cambodia, which is a necessary condition for the continued development of human capital full of skills and abilities to promote productivity, competitiveness, high economic growth, create new jobs better and maintain existing jobs. Aims to continue to improve the living standards of the people.
2: Continue to participate and provide support in the implementation of the National Policy on Technical and Vocational Education and Training in order to achieve the vision of developing Cambodia's human resources with knowledge, ability, skills, work ethics, high productivity and professionalism. Competitive characteristics for a lifetime job.
3: Continue to encourage and increase the enrollment of children in technical and vocational skills to have clear skills can seize the good job opportunities that are growing and increase family income.
4: All public and private radio and television stations, as well as the media, please continue to help spread the word about education, technical and vocational training to increase awareness of the importance of this sector among the public.
5: In particular, on the occasion of the 2nd National Day of Technical and Vocational Education and Training under the theme "TVET can be done", the relevant ministries, institutions, authorities at all levels, professors, teachers, students, parents, guardians, employers, workers, private sector, organizations Not only the government, development partners and all our compatriots are cooperating in organizing and promoting this National Day to be celebrated solemnly throughout the country.
According to the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, technical and vocational education and training play an important role in the development of human capital, knowledge, skills, work attitudes and professional ethics, which are the determining factors in promoting productivity, competitiveness and investment attractiveness. Aims to maintain high economic growth, create better new jobs, improve incomes and live in harmony.
