Doctors: Men are more likely to get kidney stones than women
(Siem Reap): Doctors who specialize in kidney stones say that kidney stones are more common in men than women. This is according to Dr. Sao Ravi, a kidney specialist from Thailand of Royal Phnom Penh Hospital.
Dr. Sao Ravi explained that the factors that cause men to be more prone to kidney stones than women are: men's lifestyle, men's eating habits mean that men eat more than women in particular. Eating outside without proper hygiene.
Dr. Sao Ravi made this remark on the occasion that the doctors of the Royal Angkor Hospital in Siem Reap and the Royal Hospital of Phnom Penh participated in a workshop on sharing knowledge to the people and advise on how to prevent birth. Kidney disease, especially kidney stones, as well as showing the effective kidney stone injection machine.
Dr. Sao Ravi said in the seminar that Cambodia has more people with kidney stones than Thailand, because people do not understand the precautionary measures and poor diet, protein balance. Eating too much salt, not drinking enough water, and limited health and nutrition education.
"It is more common in men than in women," he said. "To avoid kidney stones, people need to drink plenty of water, drink two and a half liters and get hygienic food."
Doctors expect that Cambodians will understand how to protect their health from kidney stones after attending this workshop. The doctor stated that this workshop really provides many benefits to people understand the difficulties of kidney stones and know how to prevent and treat.
Mr. Yun Sokhom, Marketing Manager of Royal Phnom Penh Hospital and Royal Angkor Hospital, Siem Reap Province, said that the workshop was organized after seeing that people have an impact on health due to kidney stones. There is a team of doctors from Royal Hospital from Thailand and Cambodia to share and provide knowledge about the effects of kidney stones and how to prevent kidney stones.
He added that kidney stones, if people understand before the disease will be easier to treat and spend less time and save money. The Royal Hospital has the latest technology to treat kidney stones easily without the use of anesthesia or acupuncture, and also does not waste time treatment.
