Hungarian PM announces support for Cambodia in EBA defense
(Hungary): The Prime Minister of Hungary, Victor Oben, who has been with the government of Hun Sen since 1979, has vowed to support Cambodia in advocating for the protection of the EBA. ) On the EU market.
Victor Oben made the announcement during a two-hour closed-door meeting with Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen in Budapest, Hungary, during his first visit as Prime Minister of Cambodia. Hold Hungary on the afternoon of June 13, 2019 (Hungarian time).
Mr. Kao Kim Hourn, Minister accompanying the Prime Minister, stated that during the meeting, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and the Prime Minister of Hungary discussed carefully the situation in the European Union, especially after the recent parliamentary elections. The Prime Ministers of Cambodia and Hungary also discussed in detail Hungary's position and views on EU policy, especially on the EBA.
According to Mr. Kao Kim Hourn, in the presence of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Hungary was most interested in the cooperation between the EU and ASEAN, which is important for Hungary.
The Prime Minister of Hungary added that Samdech Techo has played an important role in the framework of ASEAN and the European Union. In particular, promote cooperation in the fields of economy, culture and training of human resources.
Regarding the EBA issue, the Hungarian Prime Minister said that it was a matter of disagreement between the EU and Cambodia. The Prime Minister affirmed that Hungary has always stood by and supported Cambodia, especially Hungary does not support mixed political and economic issues.
The Prime Minister of Hungary also asked Samdech Techo Hun Sen to trust Hungary in advocating with the European Union on the EBA.
It should be noted that the European Commission on 12 February 2019 officially announced the start of the procedure for the temporary suspension of the preferential tariff system under the program "All goods except arms (EBA)". The procedure for the temporary suspension of the preferential system shall take up to 18 months from the date of the announcement of the procedure until the date of entry into force in case the EU does not accept Cambodia's interpretation and explanation.
Currently, the procedure for the suspension is in the first stage, the stage of Cambodia's debate and explanation to the European Commission, which is six months from the date of the European Commission announcement.
Cambodian goods exported to the EU include all kinds of clothing and footwear, rice, grains, leather goods, umbrellas, rubber and bicycles. Cambodia's exports to the EU amount to more than $ 6 billion a year.
Of the $ 6 billion in exports to Europe, Cambodia has earned nearly $ 700 million from preferential tariffs. Therefore, if Europe removes the preferential system and return to tax on Cambodian goods, it will cause Cambodia to lose nearly $ 700 million in taxes.
In March, the government and the private sector announced a series of reforms that would bring businesses more than $ 400 million in profits. Through this reform, it is believed that businesses will still be able to export their goods to the European market even without the preferential tariff system.
