US warns India over S-400 missile purchase from Russia could jeopardize defense deal
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - India's commitment to buy S-400 air defense missile system from Russia could jeopardize an arms deal between New Delhi and Washington, the State Department has warned India. This is according to a quote from the Russian newspaper Sputnik on Thursday night, June 13, 2019.
The Indian government signed a $ 5.43 billion deal to buy Russia's S-400 system in October 2018, and last week the Indian Ministry of Foreign Affairs issued a statement expressing its commitment not to give up. No agreement, despite US sanctions.
A law called CAATSA, passed by the US Congress in 2017, authorizes Washington to unilaterally impose sanctions on any country that dares to buy weapons from Russia. As India and Turkey face sanctions, the United States used the law in September 2018 to impose sanctions on China over its purchase of Russia's S-400 anti-aircraft missile system.
