(Koh Kong): In another important step towards a more beautiful, green and healthy Cambodia, NagaWorld Social Team, also known as "NagaWorld Generous Heart", has continued to cooperate with the Ministry of Environment, Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports. Teachers and students to plant 570 saplings in schools in Koh Kong province.
Together with the seedlings planted in Phnom Penh and Kampot in the previous project, NagaWorld Generous Heart has kept its promise by sponsoring 1,000 seedlings as part of the Awareness Raising Program. Environment and sapling.
Trees are an important resource in our natural ecosystem. Trees help reduce air pollution and reduce climate change. "Scientific studies have calculated that one hectare of large trees can absorb enough carbon dioxide per year to reduce the carbon footprint of 10 people."
Ms. Prum Sophy, Undersecretary of State for the Ministry of Environment, said that the 1,000 saplings planted were a remarkable achievement that improved the quality of life for students and the community. In addition to providing us with fresh oxygen, the trees provide us with cool shade and are also a haven for wildlife. Trees are very important.
Deputy Governor of Koh Kong Province, Mr. On Phearak, noted that the tree roots absorb rainwater and prevent soil erosion, which helps reduce flooding, which causes schools to close. So trees are an important element in helping us run our school, even during the monsoon season.
In particular, Mr. Nget Leung, Director of the Department of Education, Youth and Sports of Koh Kong Province, said that a greener environment will provide happiness and happiness to students who are the future of the country, so we fully support this program.
At the same time, Mr. Pho Bunthoeun, Principal of Koh Kong High School, confirmed his support and welcomed the planting of new seedlings in schools. He would like to express his deep gratitude to NagaWorld for its generous heart and the relevant ministries for co-creating this environmental program. Daily June 12, 2019 (broadcast Monday-Friday from 7:00 pm).
