3-year prison library project worth more than 530,000 dollars officially announced!
(Phnom Penh): Sipar, General Department of Prisons and Police Academy of Cambodia this morning, June 12, 2019, signed a joint agreement to officially announce the implementation of the "Prison Library Project" for 3 years (from March 2019). Until February 2022), the third phase, worth more than 530,000 dollars.
The agreement was chaired by Mr. Lim Sokha, Deputy Director General of the General Department of Prisons, Representative of General Santibandit Chan Kimseng, Director General of the General Department of Prisons and a partner in providing assistance to the French Agency for Development. The ceremony was held at the meeting hall of the General Department of Prisons.
This project will continue to strengthen existing library services and other educational services and ensure that the General Department of Prisons owns and ensures the sustainability of future projects by incorporating this project into the General Department of Prisons' new strategic plan for 2019. 2023 and train all relevant partners.
According to Prum Channy, Director General of the Department of Education, Rehabilitation and Rehabilitation, the project focuses on supporting the national library network for education and the integration of detainees into society. The project will be completed in early 2022, meaning that Sipar will completely end its support for the prison library project.
Mr. Prum Channy stated that Sipar has worked hard to bring good books for detainees to read and borrow to read in the room to give them the opportunity to study, research, increase their personal knowledge and become Good citizens to contribute to reducing crime again. Not only that, reading this book is important, including:
Please be informed that the expected results of the third phase of this prison library project:
* 35% of the 30,000 inmates will receive educational services that contribute to improving prison conditions and pre-integration skills.
* Six key officials of the Department of Prisons, presidents and deputies of 81 of the 27 prisons, will receive training on prison library management and strengthen education services (literacy classes), vocational training and pre-integration programs.
Prison officials studying at the Police Academy of Cambodia will receive an introduction to the importance of reading and the library in the prison during their studies at the Police Academy of Cambodia.
