Today, EDC signed an agreement with two companies to build a 400-megawatt black and gas power plant.
(Phnom Penh): Cambodia will soon have a 400-megawatt black and gas power plant, the solution of the head of the Royal Government, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, to end the power outage in the dry season next year. Another in Phnom Penh that has already experienced that.
The power plant will use Finland's Watsila engine and Germany's main engine, which will be built in Koh Reas commune, Lvea Em district, Kandal province.
Speaking to reporters, Mr. Keo Rattanak, Director General of Electricite du Cambodge (EDC) confirmed that to prevent next year and next year, this 400-megawatt power plant is part of the Royal Government's efforts to collect more than 1,000 More megawatts before the end of 2020.
Mr. Keo Rattanak said that the 400-megawatt project not only enhances the power supply, but also plays a role in supporting the deployment of new solar power plants in the country and improving the quality. Power supply to Phnom Penh through improved tonnage.
According to a press release from Electricite du Cambodge (EDC), the project will cost a total of 380 million US dollars, of which 355 million US dollars will be spent on the construction of the two power plants and the budget for the construction of a joint substation for the two power plants. Including other infrastructure about 25 million US dollars.
EDC has received a concessional loan of US $ 300 million from the Royal Government of Cambodia, and EDC has invested more than US $ 80 million to complete the construction of the two power plants.
According to the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Mines and Energy, Mr. Ty Norin, in addition to the installation of the plant, EDC will also have to build a network from the factory in Lvea Em district to Phnom Penh to be able to extract energy.
He said that currently Phnom Penh needs about 500 megawatts of electricity. In the dry season of 2019, due to the drought, hydropower could not operate, causing a shortage of electricity in Phnom Penh and the provinces.
Through a 400-megawatt generator plant, a 150-megawatt coal-fired power plant in Sihanoukville, and 200 megawatts of electricity from the Don Sahong hydropower plant in Laos, Mr. Tin Norin warmly said that 2020 in Cambodia in particular In Phnom Penh, there will be no shortage of electricity like in 2019.
Please be informed that recently at the graduation ceremony for students of the Royal University of Phnom Penh, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, announced that Phnom Penh will no longer face the shortage of electricity as in 2019.
According to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, this shortage is due to the Royal Government's decision to install generators that can produce up to 400 megawatts of electricity for use in Phnom Penh when hydropower lacks water.
