Documentary film "Forest Guard" that encourages everyone to understand the history and value of forest conservation will be screened on June 21 in Oddar Meanchey
(Oddar Meanchey): On June 21, 2019, there will be a screening of the documentary film "Wild Guard" at the Water Forum in front of Oddar Meanchey Provincial Hall. "Forest Guard" will encourage everyone to learn about the history and value of forest conservation, and this documentary will show the life of wildlife, natural resources and forests in community forests in Oddar Meanchey.
Anlong Veng was the last Khmer Rouge stronghold in the north of Cambodia and was the first peace zone in Cambodia.
The story of "The Forest Guard" is about the initiative and efforts of a monk named Bun Salout, who is the head of the Oddar Meanchey Provincial Priesthood to lead the conservation of the remaining forests in Oddar Meanchey.
Demonstrating the forest and Anlong Veng area was not only one of the aims of the documentary, but also to eliminate and transform the atrocities of the Khmer Rouge regime into a new national spirit that co-existed with the creation of forests. In this area as well.
"The Guardian" is an optimistic and confident film about the younger generation who will follow the example of Venerable Bun Salut, who is committed and has a national spirit in preserving Cambodia's culture and natural resources. The "Forest Guard" will bring visitors connected to the forest biodiversity.
This vulnerable forest has been officially authorized by the Royal Government of Cambodia to be preserved as a protected forest, officially named the "Sang Ruk Voan Community Forest".
Venerable Bun Salout, the head of the community, said that the community forest of Sang Ruk Voan covers a total area of 30,256 hectares, covering three districts: Samrong, Chong Kal and Anlong Veng districts. This forest is classified as a forest protected by a sub-decree of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
The above three districts are also included in the master plan prepared by the Documentation Center of Cambodia, an international member. The idea of the Anlong Veng district master plan to transform Anlong Veng into a city of peace, innovation and prosperity along the northern border.
The Documentation Center identifies 14 major historical sites along the road from Anlong Veng to the top of Dangrek Mountain. History has given a lot of meaning to these places, and even this dark history has value.
The history of the Khmer Rouge is something that makes Anlong Veng unique. The challenge is to capture the essence of the region without getting caught up in the dark past. The goal of the Documentation Center of Cambodia is to create an area that does not need to eradicate the memory of violence and trauma, but to turn that area from a place of pain into a place of rebirth.
In this way, the dark history can be a bright future, and the place of violence against women where Son Sen's wife and daughter were raped and killed could become a women's center for training and counseling.
Mine stockpiles can be turned into places where people can make crutches, wheelchairs and bicycles. In this way, places where people are injured and disabled can become places where people can work. Promote people with disabilities to be able to walk again and restore society.
By designing this master plan, we will create an unforgettable aesthetic location in the Anlong Veng community. Innovative use of natural resources, topography, forests, mountains, rivers, lakes will help maintain the balance from erosion and help develop the area in the long run.
Therefore, he volunteered to take care of this forest and submitted documents to the relevant institutions, ministries and the government to apply for the right to protect, preserve and preserve this community forest area since 2001.
He established various religious programs, including: organizing festivals, planting trees to educate and educate people in the community about the importance of forests. Today, he builds seven forest watchdogs, each with three or four patrols, including monks.
Despite the challenges of his past work, he never gave up or wanted to stop his activities because he loved nature and wanted to preserve the Prey Sang Rok Voan community area to become an attractive tourist destination. Tourists from all over come to walk in this forest.
For almost 20 years, he has worked hard to preserve the Prey Sang Ruk Voan community area, and as a result, the Prey Sang Rok Voan community has benefited the people who live and enjoy the non-timber forest products in this area, nine villages, equal to almost one. Hundreds of families.
In particular, the Prey Sang Ruk Voan community area also provides shelter for a large number of wildlife. He hopes that the Cambodian people will be more aware of the importance of forests because forests and people must be related and enjoy each other. The Sang Rok Voan community forest will be carefully protected and preserved to be a natural resource for future generations.
In the past, the Sangha Ruk Voan community forest was also under the control of the Khmer Rouge. The military, Khmer Rouge cadres, and people fleeing attacks by Vietnamese troops and the Kampuchean United Front for National Salvation in 1979 fled to gather and live in the forest.
The Sang Ruk Voan Community Forest not only maintains a beautiful natural environment, greenery and contributes to sustainable development and serves the tourism industry, but is also linked to the violence and history of the group's brutal killings. The Khmer Rouge.
The Khmer Rouge regime not only harmed people, nature and society, but also wreaked havoc on culture and religion, which continues to this day. As well as mechanisms for protecting, conserving, and demonstrating the importance of community forests, the history of the Khmer Rouge regime should be discussed and learned.
The documentary "Forest Guard" and the plot of Anlong Veng district will help Oddar Meanchey province to develop and preserve history together.
