TOP NEWS: Singapore's Foreign Minister calls Cambodian Foreign Minister to end discrimination and claims that Singapore has no intention of defending the Khmer Rouge
Foreign Minister Vivian stated that Singapore only wanted peace and prosperity for Cambodia, had no intention of supporting the Khmer Rouge and did not want to see the regime return to Cambodia. Let there be no doubt about this honesty.
The Cambodian Ministry of Foreign Affairs added that in the telephone conversation, the two foreign ministers said that despite the two countries' different historical perspectives, the two countries continue to be good neighbors and friends. Good on each other and try to forget the past to continue to maintain good conversation and communication with each other.
The two Foreign Ministers believe that the meeting between the two Prime Ministers on the occasion of the upcoming ASEAN Summit will provide an opportunity for the two sides to better understand each other and further strengthen ties and cooperation. Better relations between Cambodia and Singapore, as well as with all ASEAN member countries for peace, stability and prosperity in the region and the world.
In this press release, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Cambodia also stated that Cambodia takes a position not to open the past to harm the present and the future. The two countries work together with other countries in Southeast Asia to build a united and strong Association of Southeast Asian Nations, which is the aspiration of leaders and the people of ASEAN as a whole.
Please note that the Minister of Foreign Affairs of Singapore immediately called the Minister of Foreign Affairs of Cambodia after many reactions from the Prime Minister of Cambodia Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Cambodia and the people of Cambodia. The Prime Minister of Singapore, Lee Hsien Loong, said that Vietnam invaded Cambodia in the 1980s on May 31.
Not only Cambodia but also Vietnam reacted. The Singapore Ministry of Foreign Affairs on June 7 also issued a statement in response to the reaction. According to the statement, Singapore's Foreign Minister also called Deputy Prime Minister Pham Binh Minh, Minister of Foreign Affairs of Vietnam.
