400 MW power plant to save more than 355 million USD to install in Lvea Em district
(Phnom Penh): During the graduation ceremony for students of the Royal University of Phnom Penh on June 3, 2019, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, announced that Phnom Penh will no longer face the same power shortage as in 2019. . According to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, this shortage is due to the Royal Government's decision to install generators that can produce up to 400 megawatts of electricity for use in Phnom Penh when hydropower is short of water.
Secretary of State for the Ministry of Mines and Energy Ty Norin told Fresh News on June 4, 2019 that the generator used to generate electricity is powered by black oil and gas. The generator-powered power plant will cost more than $ 355 million to build, assemble and install.
According to Ty Norin, the generator was ordered from Finland and Germany. Mr. Ty Norin confirmed that the generator-powered power plant will be built in Lvea Em district, Kandal province.
According to the Secretary of State, the construction agreement for the plant will start on June 11 and construction will take 10 months, with completion scheduled for April 2020.
In addition to the factory installation, Electricite du Cambodge (EDC) will also have to build a network from a factory in Lvea Em district to Phnom Penh to generate energy.
Currently, Phnom Penh needs about 500 megawatts of electricity. In the dry season of 2019, due to the drought, hydropower could not operate, causing a shortage of electricity in Phnom Penh and the provinces.
Currently, Electricité du Cambodge (EDC) and the Kandal Provincial Authority are cooperating to purchase land at market value in Koh Reas commune, Lvea Em district, for the construction of this generator-powered power plant. About 90 families have been affected by the project. This is according to Mr. Phim Phira, the governor of Lvea Em district, told Fresh News today.
