Cambodia remains highly committed to sending Blue Hat soldiers to humanitarian activities around the world through the United Nations (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Senior Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation official Ouch Borith affirmed that Cambodia remains Highly committed to sending peacekeepers to humanitarian action around the world through the United Nations.
The statement was made at the closing ceremony of the Regional and ASEAN Advanced Demining Training for Trainers in the United Nations Peacekeeping Operations at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs on the morning of the 31st. May 2019.
For the past 13 years, Cambodia has sent Blue Hat forces to join peacekeeping missions under the United Nations umbrella with donor countries around the world. To date, Cambodia has sent a total of 6,278 troops to eight countries, including Sudan, South Sudan, Lebanon, Cyprus, Syria, Chad, Mali and Central Africa, including 315 women.
"Currently, our force of 788 is carrying out peacekeeping missions in five countries on demining, civil engineering, airport engineering, military engineering, observer and cadet officers," he said.
Cambodia, once a country severely affected by chronic war and once aided by UN peacekeepers, has rapidly developed into a country capable of sending troops on a UN mission to the rescue. To the countries affected by the world war.
