The handover of the Chinese man came after Phnom Penh International Airport police stopped a Chinese man named LIAO JINGQING in connection with a case of bringing $ 855,000 from Hong Kong to Cambodia. .
It should be noted that the police force of the Phnom Penh International Airport Gate Police Station, in cooperation with the Customs and Excise Office at the airport, on the evening of May 29, 2019, arrested another Chinese national at Phnom Penh International Airport, confiscating nearly 1 million dollars. The United States, where the money was imported from Hong Kong, China.
Interior Ministry officials in charge of immigration planning confirmed to Fresh News that the arrest of the Chinese national was due to the money being suspected of having money from an unknown source.
According to the preliminary interrogation, the suspect confessed that he entered Cambodia twice, the first time on May 26, 2019 and the last time on May 29, 2019, he was arrested by the authorities. The suspect has a job in China as a hotel decorator.
According to the police report, the confiscated evidences include: 1 Chinese passport, 855,000 US dollars (72 pieces), 1 mobile phone and 2 large bags. The suspect is currently being held for questioning and further proceedings.
On April 23, the Immigration Police Force and the General Department of Customs and Excise at Phnom Penh International Airport also arrested three Chinese suspects who brought in $ 3,520,000. Without specifying the exact source of financial from Hong Kong to Cambodia.
Letter to readers: Cambodia's development vision to become a thriving, environmental and sustainable society
(Phnom Penh): Cambodia after the war has testified to the great achievements of peace building, national reunification and economic development. Since achieving full peace in 1998, Cambodia has achieved remarkably high economic growth, with an average growth rate of 8% per year.
In addition, the poverty rate has fallen steadily from 53% in 2004 to only 10% in 2019. At the same time, the education and health sectors have made significant progress. The rapid socio-economic development and all the other achievements that Cambodia has achieved so far are the result of peace and stability, which have been difficult to achieve through a well-thought-out strategy for national reconciliation and peace building.
Prime Minister Hun Sen stressed at the 75th session of the United Nations Economic and Social Commission for Asia and the Pacific (UN-ESCAP) in Bangkok earlier this week that peace and stability And equitable and equitable growth is the foundation for national development.
National development is a work in progress and the Royal Government of Cambodia has launched a relentlessly innovative development strategy. For example, the Royal Government of Cambodia has initiated an ambitious development plan to transform Cambodia into a high-middle-income country by 2030 and a high-income country by 2050. Every citizen will receive the same social protection in the Social Security Network program.
Achieving inclusive and equitable growth and strong development for post-war societies is a global challenge. Cambodia is also in this challenge. On the other hand, the benefits of economic growth in Cambodia have not yet been fairly and equitably distributed. On the other hand, socio-economic inequality, which remains a development challenge, complicates access to economic opportunities in general.
Notably, rural infrastructure is still underdeveloped compared to urban areas. The combination of a weak domestic labor market has spurred migration to neighboring countries and the region.
In the spirit of leading the country towards the direction of growth and sustainable development, Cambodia needs a comprehensive and in-depth development strategy and a well-coordinated development approach. Strategies for long-term development and economic growth require the adherence to a crisis-oriented economy and society and sustainable development.
As knowledge and solutions in rural areas are still limited, building resilience and environment, the Tamro Techo 100 Village Project will play a role in resolving those crises.
The project will also create a new impetus for sustainable development in rural Cambodia through innovation development, rural governance, leadership capacity building and infrastructure development. Currently, the Asian Vision Institute (AVI) is implementing a number of pilot research projects in Preah Vihear and Takeo provinces. These projects are expected to be extended to all 25 provinces of Cambodia.
The experiences and lessons learned from this project will be shared with other villages across the country, and similar projects can be replicated and implemented in other Asian countries. Another development angle is the development of smart cities, which are an important component of Cambodia's development vision.
This vision follows the inspiration of the ASEAN Smart Cities Network (ASCN), which was established in 2018 with the aim of improving people's lives using technology. Battambang, Phnom Penh and Siem Reap are among the cities in the priority network.
