Addressing the conference, the Prime Minister said that countries in Asia, as well as the world as a whole, are facing unprecedented crises, complexities and risks, both traditional and non-traditional, for a period of time. Short and long term ahead. These challenges can hinder progress and undermine the achievements of our nation over the past decades, as well as reduce the potential for future development.
He also expressed concern about world peace, stability and security in the face of progress towards a multi-polar framework due to competition for interests and the adjustment of global power balance, as well as other hostilities that are causing uncertainty. And much chaos to the normalization of Asian socio-economic development.
With all this in mind, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, shared some views on the theme of the 25th International Conference on the Future of Asia as follows:
First, the reorganization of institutions and global governance rules based on core principles and fundamental global values: Indeed, the emergence of new economic blocs and poles requires a reorganization of institutional norms and rules. Global management to ensure the balance of power and division of interests, responsibilities and the proper and effective implementation of the duties of each character.
In a multi-polar world, it is necessary to clearly divide responsibilities, build trust and promote cooperation among the major powers, and to continue to enable the participation of developing countries as well as to further enhance their role. Of multilateral institutions to create a conducive environment for ensuring that all actors can work together to address challenges and build a new, balanced, environmentally and justly constructive world.
As a Least Developed Country, Cambodia is deeply concerned about any unilateral decision that deviates from the principles and rules of international law, as well as its obligations to international treaties and agreements. Such actions have been weakening state-to-state relations and causing tensions in the international community.
