Phnom Penh Capital Administration and Cambodia Coca-Cola Company Continue to Install a New Water Purification Tower worth Nearly $ 20,000 for Bun Rany Hun Sen High School Koh Dach
(Phnom Penh): On Wednesday, May 29, 2019, Phnom Penh Capital Administration and Cambodia Coca-Cola Company continued to respond to the drought in Cambodia by installing a new water filter tower. Nearly $ 20,000 is located on the campus of Bun Rany Hun Sen High School, Koh Dach, in Sangkat Koh Dach, Khan Chroy Changva, Phnom Penh.
Through the installation of a new water filter tower, it can provide drinking water to more than 1,100 students, teachers, as well as people living in the surrounding community. The inauguration ceremony was presided over by Mr. Nuon Pharath, Deputy Governor of Phnom Penh and Mr. Ros Sophea, Country Director of Legal and Public Affairs of Cambodia Coca-Cola.
Mr. Ros Sophea said: "Our commitment does not stop at the installation of a new water treatment tower today, but we will continue to fully support and cooperate with Planet Water to implement health education programs. "Efficient use of clean water, environmental sanitation, education and training of communities on the maintenance and protection of this water tower, including five years of recreation."
Please be informed that this is the fourth water purification tower installed by Coca-Cola with partner institutions in 2019, in addition to 18 other water purification towers that the company has installed throughout Cambodia since 2016 In collaboration with Planet Water, which has installed water filter towers in communities of Cambodia that are facing a shortage of clean drinking water.
Coca-Cola says three more water treatment towers will be installed across Cambodia in 2019, with more to come in the coming years.
The Cambodian Coca-Cola Company said that the 18 water treatment towers that the company has installed in Cambodia since 2016 have provided 44,990,286 liters of clean water to more than 40,000 communities. This figure is expected to increase 1.5 times this year with the installation of three more water treatment towers in 2019.
