New law in the Philippines: Each pre-graduate student "must plant 10 trees" to help the country's environment
(Manila): A new law in the Philippines requires all graduates to plant at least 10 trees each before they can officially graduate. This was quoted from the Chinese news agency Xinhua in the morning of Wednesday, May 29, 2019.
While each year in the Philippines, more than 12 million primary school students and nearly 5 million high school students, including nearly 500,000 students, must complete the new law if properly implemented and adhered to. Ensure that at least 175 million new saplings are planted each year, and no less than 525 billion saplings per generation.
Prior to the law, planting trees after graduation was just a tradition in the ASEAN nation, one of the world's most deforested countries. Planting trees can also be a strategy to tackle global climate change, as the Philippines is home to thousands of islands and suffers from severe typhoons every year.
