Minister of Rural Development: The Ministry will try to mobilize financial assistance to improve rural roads to paved or concrete roads
(Kampong Chhnang): A two-story DBST paved road with a total length of 35,457 km and a concrete bridge with a length of 125 meters and some artistic structures in the four target provinces of the first phase of rural road improvement project were inaugurated. Launched this morning, May 28, 2019 in Banh Chkol village, Chrey Bak commune, Rolea Bier district, Kampong Chhnang province, under the presidency of Dr. Ouk Rabun, Minister of Rural Development.
Dr. Chandarong, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Rural Development and Project Director, stated that in the contract between the Royal Government of Cambodia, represented by the Ministry of Rural Development and the Government of the People's Republic of China, a total grant of 83,648,374 RMB, equivalent to approximately US $ 12,674,000 for the implementation of the first phase of rural road improvement project for a period of 24 months from May 30, 2017 to April 10, 2019.
Dr. Ouk Rabun stated that the Royal Government under the wise leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, considered that the infrastructure policy, including: Road and bridge construction is an indispensable priority in reducing poverty. Of the people.
Minister Ouk Rabun also stated that the Ministry of Rural Development will try to mobilize financial assistance to improve the gravel roads and red gravel roads in rural areas to be paved or concrete.
The Prime Minister thanked the Government of the People's Republic of China for its continuous and timely and generous financial and technical assistance to Cambodia without any complicated conditions. Continue to assist the Ministry of Rural Development to continue to implement the second phase of the rural road improvement project.
According to a press release from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, during his visit to Japan from May 29 to 31, Samdech Techo Hun Sen attended the 25th International Conference on the Future of Asia and other events. .
At the 25th International Conference, the Prime Minister will deliver a keynote address on the theme of the conference, "Finding a New World Order - Overcoming Chaos" and sharing perspectives on the current global context, future vision, path to the future. Going forward and Cambodia's commitment and contribution to promoting harmony, peace and common prosperity in the Asia-Pacific region and in the world.
The Foreign Ministry said that after attending the conference, the Prime Minister will have a bilateral meeting with the Prime Minister of Japan Abe Shinzo to discuss bilateral, regional and international issues of common concern.
On that occasion, the two Prime Ministers will witness the signing of an exchange of letters on the grant agreement of Japan for two development projects in Cambodia, including: 1. The implementation of economic and social development programs (construction 200 million Japanese Yen (US $ 1.8 million) container terminal; and (339 million Japanese Yen) US $ 339 million human resource development scholarship project.
During his stay in Tokyo, the Prime Minister will meet with Japanese dignitaries and receive courtesy calls from representatives of many Japanese companies.
In 2018, Cambodia and Japan celebrated the 65th anniversary of the establishment of diplomatic relations, with the exchange of congratulatory messages between the Prime Minister, parties and cultural events.
Separately, the Prime Minister of Japan also visited Cambodia frequently. Mr. Abe Shinzo paid an official visit on 16-17 November 2013, Mr. Yoshihiko Noda attended the ASEAN Summit in November 2012, Mr. Yuni Hiroko Oksemi attended the ASEAN Summit in November 2002, Mr. KOBG paid an official visit on January 10-11, 2000.
Environmental official: Cambodia's GDP will decrease by 10% due to climate change by 2050
(Siem Reap): A senior official of the Ministry of Environment, Ms. Mom Thany, said that in the event that there is no solution to the problem of climate change from now on, according to estimates in the model analysis report of the National Council for Sustainable Development with Ministry of Economy and Finance Cambodia's gross domestic product (GDP) in 2050 is expected to decline by almost 10% due to climate change challenges.
Statement by Ms. Mom Thany, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment and Chair of the National Council for Sustainable Development, at the Opening Ceremony of the Intergovernmental Group on Climate Change Dissemination Workshop on the morning of 27 May 2019 In Siem Reap.
She added that these are the reasons why Cambodia has become a strong supporter of international efforts to combat global climate change. She said that although Cambodia is not historically responsible for greenhouse gas emissions, it is "very committed" to promoting a development model that is resilient to climate change and low carbon emissions.
