Heng Sour presents the pyramid of labor in Cambodia during the Fourth Industrial Revolution
(Phnom Penh): Speaking to reporters after the national high-level dialogue on the future of employment for Cambodia, Heng Sour, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, said that the labor force in Cambodia can be divided into three main groups. Shaped like a pyramid so that they can evolve in line with the Fourth Industrial Revolution and to respond to the changing world of work.
He summarized the pyramids of the main workforce as follows:
* The top of the pyramid is a group of people who are researchers of technology, scientists, inventors of machinery and equipment, etc., which are smaller than other groups. Once this group has produced something, it is the turn of the group of people in the middle of the pyramid.
* The center of the pyramid is a team of experts who can use the products of scientists. This group can convert usage from complex to simple for another group to implement.
The lower part of the pyramid is the largest group of people with little education, such as workers, employees, etc. You are trained in the use of any machine or electronics from a professional team (located in the middle of the pyramid) and must be proficient in using them in order for the production process to run smoothly.
Through the presentation of the pyramid, Mr. Heng Sour stated that the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, as well as other relevant ministries and institutions must work together to increase human resources in the middle of the pyramid, because some tools we can Buy from abroad, but must have someone who knows how to use it and can train others to use it.
Heng Sour said that Cambodia should not expect too much in order to turn all the people into scientists, Cambodia needs some people as scientists, researchers, technology, etc. to serve its economy. More people are needed to turn technology into practice.
"Besides, employed workers need to be prepared to be good technologists, I think it will be enough for Cambodia in the last 5-10 years if we know how to use them," he said. "A good technology, our children in 20 years will do better than us."
Please be informed that this National High-Level Dialogue on the Future of Jobs for Cambodia was held throughout the day on May 27, 2019 in order to establish a forum for initiating discussions between representative policy makers. Workers representing employers and development partners in Cambodia's better preparedness to respond to the changing world of work.
