Legal Awareness: Criminal Liability of Minors
(Phnom Penh): Criminal is a kind of principle that is written to punish any person who violates the law, especially the Penal Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia. Therefore, when a crime occurs, there must be someone responsible for the crime.
In principle, people who are not responsible for the seriousness of their actions cannot be held criminally responsible, because a sense of responsibility for their actions is a factor that leads individuals to be criminally liable. .
Therefore, the Cambodian Penal Code clearly defines criminal liability, in which Article 38 states that the legal age of criminal liability is 18 (eighteen) years or older. What if a minor under the age of 18 commits a crime, is he criminally liable? What are the measures for minors who have committed criminal offenses?
Therefore, in order to study the notion of criminal responsibility of minors clearly, FRESH NEWS Legal Awareness Program in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation this week, will raise the topic of "Criminal Liability". Of minors ”to share to the people and students as follows:
We know that the law stipulates that criminal liability is not limited to those who are 18 years of age or older, since those under the age of 18 are minors whose understanding is still lacking. However, this can be difficult because in the case of a minor under the age of 18 committing a serious crime.
Therefore, Article 39 of the Penal Code further stipulates that the court may impose criminal penalties on minors aged 14 (fourteen) or older if the circumstances of the offense or the personality of the minor require.
This means that a minor between the ages of 14 and 18 may be criminally liable for his actions if the circumstances of the offense or the personality of the minor require.
The Penal Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia does not explicitly define the circumstances of the offense or the personality of the minor, therefore, when encountering any case, the law gives special privileges to the court (discretion). In considering whether the juvenile is responsible for his or her criminal offense.
However, in the content of Article 39, it means that a juvenile under the age of 14 cannot be sentenced by a criminal court.
In general, all juveniles between the ages of 14 and 18 who commit all crimes may be placed on probation, education, protection and support measures:
This person regularly submits a report on the conduct of minors to the prosecutor. This person informs the prosecutor of all events that could lead to a change in this rule.
Conversely, if the court deems that the circumstances of the offense or the juvenile's personality are required, it means that the juvenile is criminally responsible for which the court must impose criminal penalties on that minor.
In this case, the Penal Code explicitly determines the penalties that can be applied to minors when the court decides to criminalize a juvenile, which is stipulated in Article 160: When the court decides to criminalize a minor under the age of 14 Four) years or more, the penalty for the offense charged is reduced in the following conditions:
1. The maximum sentence of imprisonment shall be reduced by half. 2. If the maximum sentence is life imprisonment, the maximum shall be reduced to 20 (twenty) years imprisonment. 3. The minimum sentence of imprisonment shall be halved if this minimum exceeds one (1) day. 4. The minimum and maximum fine shall be halved.
In the case of criminal charges, if the provisions of this article have the effect of reducing the maximum sentence of imprisonment to a period equal to 5 (5) years, then the charged crime shall remain the same crime. In addition to convictions, the court may also impose additional sentences and community service sentences, which are provided for in Articles 161 and 162 of the Penal Code.
In short, as long as an offense is committed, it certainly causes harm or damage, which is one of the reasons that requires the perpetrator to be held accountable for his or her actions.
However, as a separate provision, the law also sets out a separate principle of criminal liability for minors who commit crimes to avoid infringing on their rights as well as causing hardship to minors. In particular, the law on juvenile justice.
