Today, the council election, find out about the important role of the Capital, Province, Municipality, District and Khan Council
(Phnom Penh): On May 26, 2019, Cambodia organized the election of the Capital Council, Provincial Council, Municipal Council, District Council and Khan Council of the third mandate. All seven parties participating in the election are now launching their campaigns to gain support.
The election of the Capital Council, Provincial Council, Municipal Council, District Council and Khan Council shall be held in a non-universal, free, fair and just manner by secret ballot. These eligible voters are not ordinary citizens, but commune councilors elected by the people within the mandate.
What are the important messages of Capital, Province, Municipality, District and Khan Council to organize such an election?
Each council shall have a chairperson, with the chairperson being the candidate at the top of the list of candidates with the most seats on the council. In the event that the list of candidates has the same number of seats in the council, the chairperson of the council shall select the candidate who is at the top of the list of candidates with the most valid votes.
The actual number of members of each council shall be determined by sub-decree at the request of the Minister of the Ministry of Interior at least 120 days before the expiration of the term of the council. In the absence of a sub-decree stating the number of members of the next mandate council, the total number of council members for each council shall be based on the final total number of council members of the previous mandate.
Each council shall represent and act on behalf of all citizens living within its jurisdiction. Each council must establish, promote and sustain democratic development within its jurisdiction. The council has the role of managing the necessary tasks to achieve the objectives of creating, promoting and sustaining democratic development.
The council has the authority to make regulatory and executive decisions. Another special right of the council is to request the Royal Government to remove the position of the Board of Governors, for example, the provincial council has the right to request the removal of the provincial governor or deputy governor. The council shall fulfill its functions and duties by making decisions in its council meetings and shall ensure that such decisions are implemented.
The council may exercise its executive power by making decisions at council meetings. The council must be directly accountable to all citizens for priority choices to make decisions to ensure democratic development within its jurisdiction.
The council may investigate cases that it deems important to the people it represents, and may publish the results of the survey and provide guidelines to the board of governors and governors. In each of its meetings, the Council shall make decisions or orders as necessary in relation to:
The council shall prepare and approve a five (5) year development plan, which shall be updated annually in accordance with the annual three (3) year rolling investment program. In preparing and implementing the development plan, the council shall consult with:
* Citizens within their jurisdiction * Other types of councils within their jurisdiction * Ministries, relevant institutions and units of the Royal Government * Stakeholders
The council must develop a development plan that includes:
* Vision of the council, showing the goals and objectives for development within its jurisdiction.
* Estimation of existing levels of development within its jurisdiction and estimates of key development needs, including: The needs of the council in preparing for the transfer of new functions, tasks, and resources. Services, facilities, materials and public infrastructure as basic and necessary. Poverty reduction. Different needs of women, men, youth, children, vulnerable groups, including the poor, indigenous peoples.
* Key performance indicators and goals related to each development priority and direction as stated in the development plan The council shall define separately in the development plan and budget the optional functions and mandatory functions.
The council shall monitor and evaluate annually the implementation of its development plan, including the implementation plan section under the control and supervision of: * Joint councils * Other councils * Ministries Institutions, units of the Royal Government * Stakeholders.
The Council shall administer public finance to promote the development of democracy and focus on the most important development needs, including: * The need for sub-national administrations to prepare for new functions, tasks and resources * Basic and necessary public infrastructure * Poverty reduction * Other needs of women, men, youth and children Vulnerable groups, including the poor, indigenous peoples.
The council must manage its financial affairs in a transparent and accountable manner to its citizens.
Capital, provinces, municipalities and districts must have their own budget, which is called the sub-national administrative budget. Khans and Sangkats under the capital must have their own budget in the budget of the capital. Sub-districts must have their own budget in the municipal budget.
The council shall approve the budget of the sub-national administration in accordance with the procedures and calendar of the annual state budget. The council must approve the medium-term expenditure framework and update it annually. Obey traffic rules, drive carefully! 3 killed and 10 injured in traffic accidents nationwide on May 25
