(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, said that the Stung Bot International Gateway, which is planned to be built at a cost of 800 million baht, equivalent to about 26 million US dollars, is a concessional loan from the Royal Government of Thailand.
During the signing ceremony of the contract on the construction of Stung Bot International Gate and the road connecting to National Road 5 on the afternoon of May 24, 2019, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol confirmed that the construction of a new international gate will be used. 24 months.
According to Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, the construction of the Stung Bot International Gate will include an administration building, an immigration building, a customs building, temporary accommodation for officials working there and other related buildings.
The Minister of Public Works emphasized that this international gate is very important because now both traffic and tourism must be done together at Poipet International Gate, but when this gate is completed, all goods must pass through. This is except for tourism, so congestion is no more.
As for the road connecting to National Road No. 5, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol indicated that the Stung Bot area needs another 1 km road to connect from Stung Bot to National Road 5 because there is currently no road. Mr. Sun Chanthol added that according to the Royal Government's plan, there will be another flyover to bypass the road connecting National Road 5 and the road from Stung Bot to avoid traffic jams.
In addition, the infrastructure at the new international border gate will be state-of-the-art at the international level, ready for the location of a joint monitoring mechanism that can operate 24 hours a day, with temporary accommodation for officers from all walks of life. 10 minutes for inspecting a container and capable of inspecting about 100 to 500 containers per day.
