ADB provides $ 7.64 million to build 100-megawatt solar park in Cambodia
Despite significant progress in the development of Cambodia's power grid, nearly 5 million Cambodians still lack access to electricity. In 2018, Cambodia will be able to supply a total of 2,175 megawatts of electricity through the installed network, of which 1,330 megawatts or about 62% of hydropower and 780 megawatts of fossil fuels. This is according to an ADB press release.
Although Cambodia has a lot of solar energy, the current capacity to generate electricity from solar energy is only 10 megawatts. An ADB study shows that Cambodia can add about 200 megawatts of solar power to its distribution network by 2021, using existing technology and without affecting the distribution network.
The 100-megawatt solar power plant, which will be part of the National Park, will be auctioned off to an independent power producer (IPP) in two phases. 60 MW power. The ADB Public-Private Partnership Office is acting as a consultant to assist the EDC in designing and organizing an open competitive bidding process.
