30,000 saplings are planted in Angkor Park every year
(Siem Reap): APSARA Authority always takes care to maintain the existing forest and plant more in the area around the temple, which is a protected forest area due to some areas being encroached upon by forest fires and after deforestation Dead wood that can cause various dangers to people, animals, temples and the environment.
Mr. Sin Pisey, Technical Officer of the Department of Forest Management, Environment and Cultural Landscape, said that the forest in Angkor Park is divided into three main areas: Banteay Srei Park, Kandal Park and Rolous Park, covering an area of 401 square kilometers and divided into 14 posts. With a staff of 84 patrols regularly to care for and treat trees when sick, except for the Beng Mealea temple outside the Angkor Park.
He added that the forest management agency has a program to plant seedlings every year, and each time there are hundreds of trees, with the participation of the leadership of the APSARA Authority, government institutions, international institutions, the private sector, the community, monks and students. To fill the forestless area, a total of 30,000 trees per year in Angkor Park.
Mr. Sin Pisey added that in addition, APSARA Authority also distributed seedlings to various institutions, monks and people in the Angkor area to plant in their local villages to increase the number of forests, increase green cover. To the environment to have a fresh atmosphere, especially to attract tourists.
Mr. Pisey stated that forests are important both environmentally and aesthetically for humanity.
He also called on local authorities to educate people not to encroach on forest land, to plant more trees than to destroy them, and to burn the plantations properly, to control fires, to avoid accidents and risks to the forest. Angkor Park area. Ministry of Social Affairs strengthens vocational training and employment for people with disabilities to provide employment opportunities to people with disabilities
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation held a meeting on vocational training and employment for people with disabilities to provide employment opportunities and encourage people with disabilities and to promote the dignity, well-being and rights of people with disabilities in Cambodia. By recognizing the abilities of people with disabilities, it is better to see only the disabilities of people with disabilities.
This meeting was held on May 24, 2019 at the Ministry of Social Affairs with the participation of Mr. Nhek Vannara, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, representing Mr. Vong Soth, Minister of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, relevant officials from Department of Social Affairs, partners, organizations and many other collaborative institutions.
Mr. Nhek Vannara said that the current meeting on vocational training for people with disabilities is very important for the Ministry of Social Affairs and relevant partners to join forces to provide vocational training for people with disabilities in Cambodia.
Mr. Yeap Malino, Director of the Department of Welfare for the Disabled, said that at present, vocational training and employment for people with disabilities also face some problems. But in addition to facing some problems, the Ministry of Social Affairs has also made efforts to change those challenges for the better.
Mr. Yeap Malino said that vocational and vocational training for people with disabilities also found it difficult to find students with disabilities to study and train, there is not yet widespread outreach from training centers to people with disabilities and guardians. The number of people with disabilities in the community is still low.
However, people with disabilities now live on an equal footing with the general population, including employment, economic opportunities and participation in politics, society, culture and sports: people with disabilities are fully involved and the number of people with disabilities who are not Learning is declining, those who do not attend will continue to push for complementary training.
It should be noted that from 1987 to 2019, a total of 17,502 people with disabilities received vocational training in a number of occupations, including computers, garments, weavers, carpenters, electronics, repairing motorcycles, repairing machines, repairing bicycles, hairdressers, chefs, sculptors, etc. . So far, there are 2 vocational training centers, including Banteay Preap Vocational Training Center and Por Senchey Social Center, trained 663 people from 2014 to 2018.
