Government Spokesperson: Cambodia does not accept any interference in its internal affairs
(Phnom Penh): In response to a bill proposed by three US senators on May 23, 2019, Royal Government of Cambodia spokesman Phay Siphan issued a statement stating that the Royal Government of Cambodia does not Accept any interference in the internal affairs of its sovereignty.
A statement obtained by Fresh News today states: "The Royal Government of Cambodia does not accept any interference in the internal affairs of Cambodian sovereignty that it has been implementing the rule of law and national law." "And internationally, as well as contributing to the development of democracy and its civil liberties."
The Cambodian government spokesman's reaction came after a US senator proposed a new law requiring the release and dropping of charges against a former CNRP leader.
He said that the attempt to re-enact the law was a negative and poisonous negative trend against the political goodwill of the Cambodian people, who overwhelmed more than 83 percent of the electorate in July last year. 2018 voted for the Cambodian People's Party, headed by Samdech Techo, to continue to lead Cambodia in full legitimacy, as well as with the support of the national and international community.
The Spokesperson also reiterated that Cambodia pursues a foreign policy of close friendship and mutual respect in the bilateral and multilateral frameworks at the regional and international levels. This cooperation also contributes to the social and economic development.
The following is a statement from a spokesman for the Royal Government of Cambodia: The Chinese government opposes the blacklisting of Huawei and other countries that have joined the United States.
BEIJING (Reuters) - The Chinese government has strongly condemned the United States' decision to blacklist Huawei, and Beijing has opposed opposition to joining Washington. This is according to the Chinese Ministry of Commerce, quoted by Reuters on Thursday, May 23, 2019.
The Chinese Ministry of Commerce's reaction came after the United States blacklisted Chinese companies Huawei and more than 70 subsidiaries to restrict activities in the United States over national security concerns. In addition, Washington, based on national security reasons, has also urged its allies to stop using technology, especially Huawei's 5G network.
