Thank you Japanese friends for building a bridge to give birth and help repair the place where Cambodians damaged
The Tsubasa Bridge was built with the help of the Japanese government at a cost of nearly $ 100 million and was officially opened in 2015 after about five years of construction. This is the longest bridge across the Mekong River in Cambodia, providing great benefits for Cambodia in terms of both people's travel and transportation. In particular, end the misery that used to use the ferry to cross the river.
On Facebook, Cambodians wrote messages expressing their anger at the people who damaged the bridge. They said that the Japanese government is trying to build a bridge to use and should not take care of each other while driving to avoid causing damage to the bridge.
Along with this reaction, the public also expressed doubts that the bridge has been damaged for many months, but did not see professional officials to inspect or repair.
Answering this question, the official of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport confirmed that the technical team of JICA together with the officials of the Department of Public Works of Prey Veng Province has already inspected and plans to go to repair in the near future.
"Overall, the Department of Highways, Bridges and Investment of the General Department of Public Works has cooperated with JICA representatives to inspect the site, inspect, evaluate and discuss many times, and also agreed on technical work, safety, construction in cooperation. From the Prey Veng Public Works Department as well. Mr. Sothea Yut added.
The spokesman also said that the site was damaged and there was a power line running nearby, so the specialized department has already contacted and requested the cooperation of Prey Veng Electricity Authority to temporarily turn off the electricity during the operation. Coming soon. Currently, the specialized department is preparing a technical team to carry out the repair work soon.
