The Minister thanked the Royal Government and the people of Japan through JICA, which has always provided support and financial and technical support for the development of the agricultural sector in Cambodia in the past.
The Minister informed the guests that in the new context of economic growth, population, tourism and especially climate change, the Ministry has to change its strategy and goals from production to supply domestic demand for production, processing and export.
Regarding the Sorghum Silage project, the Minister supported and encouraged, stating that with the current development of agricultural technology, the use of almost all agricultural machinery has diverted farmers from raising cattle for a living. The move has helped them earn extra income while the ministry is boosting animal and animal feed production.
He added that at present, production is still limited, supply is not enough to meet market demand, the main problem at the moment is the means and cost of transportation, along with the lack of irrigation water. In particular, considering the potential of the agricultural sector in Cambodia, the company also intends to invest in this type of grass to produce compost to serve the agricultural sector in the future.
Therefore, in order for the project to be successful and contribute to the development of a sustainable and sustainable agricultural sector, the Minister requested Mr. Yuji SEGOSHE and ANP to examine the possibility of expanding the implementation of the project more widely:
First, organize a production community to increase production to ensure supply.
Second, increase the dissemination of varieties and techniques for growing this type of grass widely to farmers and farms.
Third, shift the supply target to supply in the production area to reduce the cost of transportation.
Fourth, cooperate with the General Department of Agriculture and the General Department of Animal Health and Animal Production to increase cultivation, especially in the highlands, to help prevent dehydration and improve soil quality.
Fifth, set up and complete the production chain from cultivation to full trade, such as cultivation, breeding and self-supply of the market.
