A documentary about a forest guard on the forest protection activities of a monk has received a lot of attention
(Phnom Penh): The Documentation Center of Cambodia has screened a documentary about a forest guard on forest protection activities by a Cambodian monk who left a pagoda out of love for natural resources.
The story of the forest guard shows the activities of Venerable Bun Salout in organizing a campaign to protect forests and wildlife in Anlong Veng district from the encroachment of loggers, hunters, wildlife and aquatic animals, as well as illegal mining.
The monk resisted threats and intimidation by mobilizing other monks, as well as leaving the pagoda to live peacefully in the forest. In this documentary, Venerable Bun Salout describes why he decided to become a forest ranger and how Buddhist principles support the protection of the environment.
The area under the care of the Venerable now covers a total area of more than 32,254 hectares. This forest area is known as "Prey Sang Ruk Vorn" in Anlong Veng district, the last stronghold of the Khmer Rouge resistance movement and surviving Khmer Rouge leaders in Oddar Meanchey province, Cambodia.
The screening of the first forest guard film on the afternoon of May 20, 2019 received a lot of attention from monks at all levels, professors, teachers, students and Cambodians of all walks of life. Apparently, the seats for watching this movie are in short supply because the number of visitors exceeds expectations.
After the screening, Pisey commented that Venerable Bun Salout's real film had such a good meaning that he could be considered an example to many other nature-loving monks. He abandoned the pagoda, a safe place to live in a forest full of danger.
Mr. Pisey confirmed that "this is a kind of document that shows the dedication of a monk who is the provincial priest. The provincial multiplier, if considered, is equal to the provincial governor. So maybe another monk lives in a pile of happiness in a well-arranged hut with air conditioning, but he sacrificed his physical and mental strength to fight to protect all of our natural resources, not only that monk was abused. Deforestation, catching wild animals is even more hated. ”
Although the story of the forest guard is well documented, Ms. Mey said that the screening at Wat Lanka seems to be a bit lacking because the screening place at Sala Chan is not very ventilated. From the point of view of the screening, Ms. Mey assessed that some viewers were upset because of the hot and humid atmosphere. In addition, she regrets that such a good film was shown in a cramped place.
"There are still some gaps in the preparation because we set up at the school, the atmosphere is windy, making the participants feel anxious and the sound system is not very good, the latter can not hear very well," she said. So it makes some participants feel confused in focusing on this document.
"The visit seems to be an encouragement to the producers as we express our support for the production of such documents in the future," he said. "If the production of the first document does not have the support of the producers to discourage the production of such documents for future generations to see."
The reason why Venerable Bun Salut is willing to love nature is explained that he must find something that needs immediate attention. The Buddha encouraged Venerable Bun Salout to protect the forest and wildlife.
"The Buddha understood that forests and animals can adapt and live with people, but people never learn to live in peace with them," he said. "Throughout his life, he encouraged people to love and respect the forest and other living things." Tbong Khmum Cam Control Officer Inspects Food Quality at Chimon Market in Ponhea Krek District
