A group of nearly 200 cyclists came together to bring study materials to help more than 200 students in Tycoon Island
(Phnom Penh): A group of about 200 cyclists rode their bikes from the Sokha Hotel last weekend to help 248 students at the Oknha Tey Island School in Oknha Tey Village, Khsach Kandal District, Kandal Province.
These cyclists distributed study materials to 248 students, each of whom received a school bag, two books, two pens, two pencils, a drill bit and a budget of 5,000 riels. Separately, 8 teachers received two books, two pens, a drill bit, a line of 20,000 Riels and a grant of 400,000 Riels to Chong Koh Oknha Tey Primary School.
Mr. Chhean Sovanratana, President of the Khmer Volunteer Youth Association, advised the students to study hard, stay away from drugs and to continue their charitable activities and think more about health as well as this charity cycling program.
Mr. Chhean Sovanratana said that the idea of creating this charity cycling program was made in order to contribute to the promotion of education in Cambodia and help students who lack learning materials, as well as reduce the cost of parents, including cycling. For health, stay away from drugs and share knowledge and experience to young people to gain experience in cycling as a group and improve the cycling industry to become more popular.
(Battambang): Four other former activists of the former Cambodia National Rescue Party (CNRP) testified at the Battambang Provincial Prosecutor's Office this morning, May 21, 2019, regarding the case against and violation of the Supreme Court ruling. Prosecution dissolves the CNRP.
It should be noted that Mr. KER Bunnara, a representative of the Battambang Provincial Prosecutor's Office, issued a series of orders summoning 26 former activists of the former Cambodia National Rescue Party (CNRP) in Battambang province to testify at the Battambang Provincial Prosecutor's Office this May in connection with cases of protest and abuse. Supreme Court Order issued on November 16, 2017.
Former opposition activists include Chea Chiv, Sin Chanpov Rochet, former O'Char commune chiefs, former Toul Ta Ek commune chiefs, Mak Ra and Khuon Chamroeun, former Chamkar Samrong commune chiefs and former Battambang provincial councilors.
The summons came after former lawmakers and former CNRP activists in Battambang province released a number of videos in support of Sam Rainsy's activities abroad.
